---
import PlatformLayout from '../../layouts/PlatformLayout.astro';
import SettingCard from '../../components/SettingCard.astro';
import WarningCallout from '../../components/WarningCallout.astro';
import CrossLinkChip from '../../components/CrossLinkChip.astro';
import FactCard from '../../components/FactCard.astro';
---

<PlatformLayout
  title="Amazon"
  description="Amazon knows what you buy, what you almost bought, what you say in your home, and what your neighbors put on their doorbell. Here are the settings that matter and what they actually do."
  subtitle=""
  category="E-Commerce & Smart Home Platform"
  lastVerified="March 23, 2026"
  settingsCount={10}
  severity="critical"
  severityText="Amazon operates across retail, smart home devices, cloud infrastructure, and advertising. Purchase history reveals behaviors that other platforms can only infer. Alexa devices record and store voice activity inside users' homes. Ring cameras have formalized law enforcement data sharing relationships. Amazon Sidewalk creates a mesh network using customer hardware without prominent disclosure."
  owner="Amazon.com Inc."
  ownerHQ="Seattle, WA"
  headquarters="Seattle, WA"
  monthlyUsers="~300M active buyers"
  relatedPractices={[
    { label: 'Off-Platform Activity Tracking', href: '/practices/off-platform-tracking' },
    { label: 'Ad Personalization & Targeting', href: '/practices/ad-personalization' },
    { label: 'Third-Party Data Sharing', href: '/practices/third-party-sharing' },
    { label: 'Location Data', href: '/practices/location-data' },
  ]}
>

  <!-- WHAT THEY COLLECT -->
  <div slot="what-they-collect">
    <div class="fact-grid">
      <FactCard
        label="Purchase history and browsing behavior"
        text="Every item you view, add to a wishlist, add to cart, purchase, or return is logged. Browsing behavior on Amazon includes dwell time on product pages, price comparison patterns, and abandoned cart contents."
        severity="critical"
      />
      <FactCard
        label="Voice recordings from inside your home"
        text="Alexa devices record audio beginning at the wake word and transmit recordings to Amazon servers. Recordings are stored by default and may be reviewed by Amazon employees for voice recognition improvement."
        severity="critical"
      />
      <FactCard
        label="Continuous location data from Ring and Echo"
        text="Ring doorbells and cameras capture video of your home exterior and visitors. Echo devices log when they are activated and by whom. Combined, these provide a behavioral pattern of household activity."
        severity="high"
      />
      <FactCard
        label="Amazon Sidewalk uses your device bandwidth"
        text="Amazon Sidewalk is a mesh networking feature that uses your Echo or Ring device to create a shared network with neighbors' devices. It is enabled by default on most Echo and Ring hardware."
        severity="high"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="collect-detail">
      <p>Amazon collects two categories of data that most platforms cannot access: actual purchase behavior (not inferred intent but confirmed transactions) and in-home device activity. Purchase history is among the most valuable data in advertising because it reflects real behavior rather than modeled probability.</p>

      <p>The Amazon Ads network is one of the largest digital advertising platforms, rivaling Google in some verticals. It uses purchase history, browsing behavior, and Prime membership activity to target ads both on Amazon properties and across the open web through Amazon DSP.</p>

      <p>Ring cameras have formalized relationships with law enforcement. Amazon has disclosed that it provides Ring footage to law enforcement agencies in response to emergency requests, defined broadly, without requiring a warrant or user notification in some circumstances.</p>

      <p>Fire TV devices perform app-level tracking at the OS level. Unlike traditional <a href="/practices/acr">Automatic Content Recognition (ACR)</a>, Fire TV does not use screen fingerprinting — it does not scan what is displayed on screen. Instead, it monitors all app usage across the device: which apps are opened, how long they are used, and what is watched via antenna. This means every third-party app on the device is subject to OS-level observation, regardless of the app's own privacy settings.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- SETTINGS THAT MATTER -->
  <div slot="settings">
    <SettingCard
      name="Alexa Voice History"
      severity="critical"
      defaultState="On"
      webUrl="https://www.amazon.com/alexa-privacy/apd/rvh"
      webPath={['Alexa Privacy', 'Review Voice History']}
      description="Amazon stores every Alexa voice recording by default. These recordings are used to improve voice recognition accuracy and may be reviewed by Amazon employees or contractors. The recordings are linked to your Amazon account and stored indefinitely unless manually deleted. Amazon offers automatic deletion options (3 months or 18 months) under Manage Your Alexa Data."
      whatItDoesnt="Enabling automatic deletion removes stored recordings on a schedule. It does not prevent Amazon from processing voice commands in real time. Amazon retains some data from voice interactions for fraud prevention and account security even when automatic deletion is enabled. The device continues to listen for the wake word at all times."
    />

    <SettingCard
      name="Interest-Based Ads (Amazon DSP)"
      severity="critical"
      defaultState="On"
      webUrl="https://www.amazon.com/adprefs"
      webPath={['Your Account', 'Advertising Preferences']}
      description="Amazon uses your purchase history, browsing behavior, and Prime Video watch history to serve targeted ads on Amazon and on third-party websites and apps through Amazon DSP. This is distinct from ads within Amazon search results. Amazon DSP ads follow you to news sites, apps, and other unrelated destinations using your Amazon behavioral profile."
      whatItDoesnt="Opting out of interest-based ads stops Amazon from using your behavioral profile for off-Amazon ad targeting. You will still see ads on Amazon based on the products you search for and view. Amazon continues to collect your behavioral data; it simply stops applying it to external ad campaigns."
    />

    <SettingCard
      name="Fire TV Device Usage Data"
      severity="critical"
      defaultState="On"
      path="Settings > Preferences > Privacy Settings > Device Usage Data"
      description="Amazon collects data on how you navigate the Fire TV interface — home screen browsing, settings changes, app selections. Used for marketing and product improvement."
      whatItDoesnt="Turning this off stops marketing and product improvement use of this data. Amazon still collects operational data required to run the device."
    />

    <SettingCard
      name="Fire TV App and Over-the-Air Usage Data"
      severity="critical"
      defaultState="On"
      path="Settings > Preferences > Privacy Settings > Collect App and Over-the-Air Usage Data"
      description="Amazon tracks which third-party apps you open, how long you use them, and what you watch via antenna. This is Amazon's version of viewership tracking — not traditional ACR (no screen fingerprinting), but OS-level monitoring of app activity across every app on the device."
      whatItDoesnt="Turning this off does not affect data collection from Amazon's own services (Prime Video). It also does not stop individual apps from collecting data under their own policies."
    />

    <h3 class="severity-group-label">High</h3>

    <SettingCard
      name="Ring Camera Law Enforcement Sharing"
      severity="high"
      defaultState="Amazon may share without explicit consent in emergencies"
      description="Amazon Ring has disclosed that it shares camera footage with law enforcement in response to emergency requests without requiring a warrant or notifying the device owner. The definition of emergency is determined by Amazon. Users can control who they share footage with voluntarily, but emergency sharing operates outside user control."
      whatItDoesnt="Adjusting Ring privacy settings controls voluntary sharing — who you choose to share clips with and whether your clips appear in the Neighbors feed. It does not prevent Amazon from complying with emergency requests or legal process. There is no user-facing toggle to opt out of emergency law enforcement sharing."
    />

    <SettingCard
      name="Amazon Sidewalk"
      severity="high"
      defaultState="On"
      description="Amazon Sidewalk is a shared mesh network that uses the bandwidth and hardware of your Echo, Ring, and other compatible devices to extend connectivity for nearby Sidewalk-enabled devices. It was enabled by default for most existing Echo and Ring customers in 2021 without prominent notification. The network uses a small portion of your internet bandwidth and your device hardware to serve as a relay node for others."
      whatItDoesnt="Turning off Sidewalk stops your device from participating in the shared network. It does not affect Amazon's ability to collect data from your own device usage. If neighbors' Sidewalk devices remain active, they may still use infrastructure near your home."
    />

    <SettingCard
      name="Personalized Ads on Amazon"
      severity="high"
      defaultState="On"
      webUrl="https://www.amazon.com/adprefs"
      webPath={['Your Account', 'Advertising Preferences', 'Personalized Ads Preferences']}
      description="Amazon uses your search queries, product views, purchase history, and Prime Video watch history to select which ads appear in Amazon search results, on product pages, and within Prime Video. This is the in-platform targeting system, separate from the DSP network that extends to third-party sites."
      whatItDoesnt="Turning off personalized ads on Amazon switches you to contextual ads — ads related to the products you are currently searching for rather than your historical behavior. It does not reduce Amazon's data collection. Your purchase and browsing history continues to be stored and used for other purposes."
    />

    <SettingCard
      name="Fire TV Manage Sharing From Apps"
      severity="high"
      defaultState="On (new as of 2025)"
      path="Settings > Preferences > Privacy Settings > Manage Sharing From Apps"
      description="Controls whether third-party apps can send viewing and content information back to Amazon."
      whatItDoesnt="Only controls what apps share with Amazon. Does not affect what those apps collect independently or share with their own partners."
    />

    <h3 class="severity-group-label">Medium</h3>

    <SettingCard
      name="Alexa Voice Profile (Voice ID)"
      severity="medium"
      defaultState="Off by default"
      description="Voice ID creates a biometric voice profile linked to your Amazon account, enabling Alexa to recognize who is speaking and personalize responses. The biometric voice data is stored on Amazon servers."
      whatItDoesnt="If Voice ID was previously enabled and is now disabled, the stored voice profile should be deleted per Amazon policy. Review Alexa Privacy settings to confirm deletion."
    />

    <SettingCard
      name="Alexa Skill Data Access"
      severity="medium"
      defaultState="Varies by skill"
      description="Third-party Alexa skills — games, productivity tools, media integrations — request permissions to access account data, smart home devices, or the ability to send notifications. Each skill operates under its own developer's privacy policy, not Amazon's. Skills can request access to your name, email, phone number, and customer profile."
      whatItDoesnt="Disabling a skill revokes future access but does not delete data that skill has already collected. The skill developer retains data per their own policy."
    />
  </div>


    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">Interest-Based Ads (Amazon DSP)</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>amazon.com &rarr; Account &amp; Lists &rarr; Your Account &rarr; Advertising Preferences.</li>
        <li>Select "Do not personalize ads from Amazon for this browser."</li>
        <li>Also accessible directly at amazon.com/adprefs.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">Amazon Sidewalk</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>Alexa app &rarr; More &rarr; Settings &rarr; Account Settings &rarr; Amazon Sidewalk.</li>
        <li>Toggle off Amazon Sidewalk.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">Personalized Ads on Amazon</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>amazon.com &rarr; Account &amp; Lists &rarr; Your Account &rarr; Advertising Preferences &rarr; Personalized Ads Preferences.</li>
        <li>Select "Do not personalize ads from Amazon."</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">Ring Camera Privacy</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>Ring app &rarr; Control Center &rarr; Video Privacy Settings.</li>
        <li>Review and disable voluntary sharing settings including Neighbors feed participation.</li>
        <li>Note: emergency law enforcement sharing cannot be disabled via user settings.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">Fire TV Device Usage Data</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>Fire TV remote &rarr; Settings &rarr; Preferences &rarr; Privacy Settings &rarr; Device Usage Data.</li>
        <li>Toggle off.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">Fire TV App and Over-the-Air Usage Data</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>Fire TV remote &rarr; Settings &rarr; Preferences &rarr; Privacy Settings &rarr; Collect App and Over-the-Air Usage Data.</li>
        <li>Toggle off.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">Fire TV Manage Sharing From Apps</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>Fire TV remote &rarr; Settings &rarr; Preferences &rarr; Privacy Settings &rarr; Manage Sharing From Apps.</li>
        <li>Toggle off.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">Alexa Skill Permissions</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>Alexa app &rarr; Skills &amp; Games &rarr; Your Skills.</li>
        <li>Review each enabled skill and tap Settings to view its permissions.</li>
        <li>Disable skills you no longer use.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- WHO GETS YOUR DATA -->
  <div slot="who-gets-data">
    <dl class="data-recipients">
      <div class="recipient">
        <dt>Amazon Advertising (DSP)</dt>
        <dd>Amazon's demand-side platform serves ads on thousands of third-party websites and apps using purchase history and behavioral data. Publishers and advertisers access targeting capabilities without receiving individual user data directly.</dd>
      </div>

      <div class="recipient">
        <dt>Alexa Skill Developers</dt>
        <dd>Third-party developers whose Alexa skills you have enabled. Each has access to the specific permissions you granted and operates under their own privacy policy.</dd>
      </div>

      <div class="recipient">
        <dt>Ring Law Enforcement Partners</dt>
        <dd>Law enforcement agencies that submit emergency requests for Ring footage. Amazon discloses this sharing in its guidelines but the threshold for emergency designation is determined internally.</dd>
      </div>

      <div class="recipient">
        <dt>IMDb TV / Prime Video Ad Partners</dt>
        <dd>Advertisers running campaigns on Prime Video and Freevee receive targeting based on viewing history and purchase behavior.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <!-- WHAT THEY KNOW -->
  <div slot="what-they-know">
    <p>Amazon's purchase data reveals behaviors that social media can only approximate. After several years of Amazon use: your approximate income range (from purchase patterns and Prime membership tier), your health concerns (from supplements, OTC medications, and health device purchases), your children's ages and interests (from toy and clothing purchases), your relationship milestones (engagement rings, baby registries, moving supplies), your financial stress patterns (buy-now-pay-later usage, downgrade in purchase quality), and if you use Alexa, the daily rhythms of your household — when people wake up, when they leave, when they return, and what music plays in the background.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- RELATED PRACTICES -->
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-practices" href="/practices/off-platform-tracking" label="Off-Platform Activity Tracking" type="practice" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-practices" href="/practices/ad-personalization" label="Ad Personalization" type="practice" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-practices" href="/practices/third-party-sharing" label="Third-Party Data Sharing" type="practice" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-practices" href="/practices/location-data" label="Location Data" type="practice" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-practices" href="/practices/dark-patterns" label="Dark Patterns" type="practice" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-practices" href="/rights/ccpa" label="CCPA (deletion rights)" type="practice" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-practices" href="/practices/acr" label="Automatic Content Recognition (ACR)" type="practice" />

  <!-- RELATED PLATFORMS -->
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-platforms" href="/platforms/google" label="Google" type="platform" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-platforms" href="/platforms/meta" label="Meta / Instagram" type="platform" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-platforms" href="/platforms/vizio" label="VIZIO" type="platform" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-platforms" href="/platforms/roku" label="Roku" type="platform" />

</PlatformLayout>

<style>
  .fact-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
  }

  .collect-detail {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  p {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .severity-group-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 1.75rem 0 0.75rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.375rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .severity-group-label:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .turnoff-group {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .turnoff-group:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .turnoff-heading {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .turnoff-steps {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.375rem;
  }

  .turnoff-steps li {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .data-recipients {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .recipient {
    padding: 1rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .recipient:first-child {
    padding-top: 0;
  }

  .recipient:last-child {
    border-bottom: none;
    padding-bottom: 0;
  }

  .recipient dt {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    margin-bottom: 0.375rem;
  }

  .recipient dd {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.65;
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
